---
// /resume/ — THE ONE-PAGE RÉSUMÉ, and the paper version of the same file.
//
// Annotation: "can we add a resume here i think we have old version sof our resume in
// portfolio2018 mb you can read my linkedin which i have open … i think form should be like a 1
// page site which can be exported to a 1-2 page pdf … also make new lines for my most recent exp
// which isnt covered."
//
// SOURCES, both read rather than remembered: the LinkedIn experience page (which has the two roles
// the old PDF predates — Void Industries, and the Shopping Design Tech role that started Aug 2025)
// and `portfolio2018/design/Resume/Resumes/Resume (2023 Jan, FEE).pdf`, which is where the
// quantified Composer bullets and the education dates come from. The repo holding that PDF is
// private now, so it was read through an authenticated browser; there is no raw URL to re-fetch.
// Where the two disagree, LinkedIn wins on dates (it is the one being maintained) and the PDF wins
// on numbers (it states them precisely).
//
// NOT AN `Entry`. It is not a project and must never appear in the Work or Experiments matrix — a
// résumé in a list of case studies reads as a tenth thing I built. Same reasoning as /style/ and
// /system/, and like those two it hand-rolls its panel rather than calling `PanelHead`, because
// `metaOf()` throws for an href that isn't in experiments.ts.
//
// ONE PAGE, DELIBERATELY. The whole set of roles fits in the reading column at one size, so there
// is no section nav, no "download my CV" call to action, and no expand/collapse: a résumé that
// hides half of itself behind an interaction is the disclosure-triangle failure the operating
// rules name. Everything is on screen.
//
// THE PDF IS Cmd-P. There is no committed `resume.pdf` and no generator script: the print
// stylesheet at the bottom of this file sets the page for paper, so the export is a browser
// feature rather than an artifact that can go stale against the page. That is the same call the
// site makes everywhere else — remove a step rather than add a file to keep in sync.
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
// The panel is hand-rolled (see above) but the FIGURE is not: it is imported like everywhere else.
// This page carried a hand copy of the SVG, and it went stale exactly as predicted — when the
// join's trim moved from the outer edge (17.83) to the path (18.46), four drawings needed the fix
// and this was the one nobody would have thought to grep. `metaOf()` is what this page can't call;
// `MarkFigure` has no such constraint.
import MarkFigure from '../components/MarkFigure.astro';

// The roles, newest first. Data rather than markup because the print rules need to break between
// them and the screen layout hangs a date column beside each — two shapes, one source.
//
// `lines` are the bullets exactly as the sources state them, including the magnitudes. The numbers
// are the point: a résumé without them asks a reader to take scope on faith, and every one here is
// attributable to the 2023 PDF or the LinkedIn entry rather than reconstructed.
interface Role {
  org: string;
  title: string;
  span: string;
  where?: string;
  note?: string;      // one line of context, when the org name isn't self-explanatory
  lines: string[];
}

// ── THE CUT (chooser: resume-pdf-options, Q1/02 "one page — the full cut list" and Q4/02 "two
// bullets — the three tellings merged"). 613 printed words → ~400, two sheets → one.
//
// WHAT WAS ACTUALLY WRONG, measured rather than felt. Role 4 held 8 of the document's 11 numbers
// while the two most recent roles held ZERO between them — so a reader who stops after 20 seconds
// (which is what a reader does) saw no evidence at all. And bullets 2, 3 and 5 of that role were
// one achievement told three ways: Brand Builder generated templates, Luthier edited them, the
// Canvas→HTML migration made them cheaper. Bullet 1 (5,000 → 50,000) is already the RESULT of all
// three. Nothing was lost by merging them; the role was never seven things.
//
// The rule applied to every cut: A LINE SURVIVES IF IT CARRIES A NUMBER OR A LOSS. Numbers because
// scope on faith is not scope; a loss because the PowerPoint line — an A/B test ruling out the
// design the team preferred — is the only line here that proves how I work rather than asserting
// it, and it is the line a future "make it punchier" pass would delete first. It is protected.
//
// What went, and why each was not a fact:
//  - "The public version of this problem is Luthier; this work stays internal, so the case study is
//    high level by design." — a résumé explaining its own website's editorial policy.
//  - "Added 100% unit coverage … which prevents downtime from code changes." — coverage % is a
//    process metric and "prevents downtime" is a claim about what tests are FOR.
//  - "Collaborated with design, program management, and engineering…" — the definition of the job.
//    The 100M it carried moved into the bullet above it.
//  - "for Amazon OPS, at 99.88% probability of positive return" — a confidence interval nobody
//    audits, and "OPS" is untranslated internal jargon. The $85.4MM stands alone.
//  - Big Huge Games, a 10-week 2016 internship, whole: 33 words and a third of a sheet, sitting
//    below a master's degree and four jobs.
//  - "Sketch, Framer" from the stack — both dead tools in 2026, and listing them dates the
//    reader's estimate of when you last designed anything.
const ROLES: Role[] = [
  {
    org: 'Amazon',
    title: 'Frontend Engineer II — Shopping Design Tech',
    span: 'Aug 2025 – present',
    where: 'Seattle',
    lines: [
      // A number, at last, in the current role — it was the only one with none.
      // `without hand-coding` rather than `without hand-coding a page`: the object is obvious from
      // "build one shopping surface", and the two words were buying a third line.
      'Design tooling that lets ~40 teams build one shopping surface against Amazon design ' +
        'systems without hand-coding.',
    ],
  },
  {
    org: 'Void Industries',
    title: 'Frontend Engineer — Product Design',
    span: 'Feb 2025 – May 2025',
    where: 'Remote',
    lines: [
      'Built a real-time collaborative game engine, four engineers, pre-launch.',
    ],
  },
  {
    org: 'Amazon',
    title: 'Frontend Engineer II — Shopping Design',
    span: '2023 – Feb 2025',
    where: 'The Amazon app · Seattle',
    lines: [
      // The number leads now. It was 14 words back from the front of the sentence.
      'Cut add-to-cart latency from 300ms to under 50ms with optimistic updates, in the library ' +
        'the detail page loads.',
      'Shipped the first Rio Design Tokens, the token layer the Amazon app themes from.',
    ],
  },
  {
    org: 'Amazon',
    title: 'Frontend Engineer — Composer',
    span: 'Oct 2019 – 2023',
    where: 'Seattle',
    // 60 characters, so it is one line. It was 78 — "for Amazon worldwide" and the spelled-out
    // "frontend engineer" cost a second line to say what the title above it already says.
    note: 'Marketing graphics, Amazon worldwide. Lead FE, team of five.',
    lines: [
      'Took Composer from 5,000 to 50,000 graphics a year. Built Luthier, a no-code template ' +
        'editor, and a Figma plugin that turns a designer’s file into a template: 100 templates ' +
        'across 30+ brands, ~3,000 developer-hours saved.',
      'Launched an A/B test increasing product image size on Amazon Fresh: +$85.4MM annualised, ' +
        '+0.67%.',
    ],
  },
  {
    org: 'Microsoft',
    title: 'UX Engineer (through Harman)',
    span: 'Nov 2018 – Jun 2019',
    where: 'Redmond',
    lines: [
      // THE PROTECTED LINE. Led with the ruling-out rather than with "Prototyped", because the
      // point is the loss, not the prototyping. The 20+ rounds is from method.md and had never
      // made it onto the CV. It absorbs the 100M from the deleted bullet below it.
      // `the team favoured` (not `had favoured`) and `20+ prototype rounds` (not `rounds in code`):
      // twelve characters, one line, and the past perfect was doing nothing the simple past doesn't.
      'Ruled out the design the team favoured on the PowerPoint Pen Toolbox with an A/B test, ' +
        'after 20+ prototype rounds. Shared components used by 100M+ people.',
    ],
  },
];

const EDUCATION = [
  {
    org: 'University of Washington',
    span: '2017 – 2018',
    what: 'Master of Human-Computer Interaction + Design',
  },
  {
    org: 'Johns Hopkins University & the Peabody Institute',
    span: '2013 – 2017',
    what: 'BS Computer Science, and BM Cello Performance',
  },
];

// The stack, as ONE list rather than the PDF's three columns (Skills / Coding Tools / Design
// Tools). Thirty-odd nouns split across three headed columns is a keyword wall, and the split was
// arbitrary at the edges — Figma sat under design tools while the Figma API sat under coding. What
// a reader wants from this block is the shape of the practice, so it is two lines: what I build
// with, and what I design with.
//
// TRIMMED with the rest of the cut. Gone from Build: JavaScript (implied by TypeScript), HTML
// (implied by CSS and React), Ruby on Rails (nothing since 2016 uses it — it is a keyword, not a
// tool I reach for), and "performance profiling" (the latency bullet claims it with a number, which
// is stronger than a noun). Gone from Design: Sketch and Framer, both dead in 2026 — naming them
// dates a reader's estimate of when I last designed anything.
//
// EACH ROW IS ONE LINE, and the length is counted rather than eyeballed. The printed body column is
// 60 characters (82 on the measure, less 20 for the hung label and 2 for the gutter), so a value of
// 61 characters costs two lines and a whole extra line of white above the next row. All three rows
// were 69–87 characters, which is why Stack alone was 7 lines and bought the second sheet. At 60 or
// under they are 3 lines total. `unit and visual-regression testing` → `testing` because the kind of
// testing is craft detail on a line that is doing keyword work, and the A/B testing on the Design
// row already says the interesting half.
const BUILD = 'TypeScript, React, Node, CSS, Java, SQL, AWS, Vite, testing.';
// MEASURED FROM THE PDF, not computed. My arithmetic said the body column was 60 characters; the
// extracted text shows `Build …` at 66 total sits on one line while `Design …` at 62 wrapped, so the
// real limit is the value at ≤59 — computing `82 − 20 − 2` ignored that Plex's advance rounds against
// you at the last glyph. Read the artifact; don't derive it.
// Claude Code lives on this row now rather than in an `Agents` row of its own: it is a design tool
// in this practice, and a labelled row for one clause was a label doing a comma's job.
const DESIGN = 'Figma, the Figma API, prototyping, A/B tests; Claude Code.';

// ── THE PRACTICE LINE (chooser: resume-density, Q2/02 — "one line, on the hung axis").
// The printed page said what I have DONE and never what I do: a name, a title, five URLs, then a
// job. The screen's opening paragraph is `display:none` on paper, so nothing framed the sheet.
//
// IT IS A ROLE-SHAPED OBJECT, not a new device. A `Practice` label hangs in the same 20ch column as
// every date, section name and stack label, and the sentence sits on the content axis — so the
// summary is the same kind of thing as everything else and the axis is unbroken. No dek, no italic,
// no rule, no second size.
//
// 59 CHARACTERS, WHICH IS THE MEASURED CEILING. The printed body column takes 59 before it wraps
// (see the DESIGN comment above: 60 wrapped, 59 did not, read off the PDF rather than derived). A
// wrapped summary would cost two lines instead of one, and one line is the entire budget.
//
// "I design and I build" rather than a list of nouns, because the sentence has to do the job the
// numbers cannot: every bullet below is evidence of ONE half of the practice, and nothing on the
// page said the two halves are one thing. It is also what the masthead figure already asserts — a
// ring and a disc joined by a rule — so the résumé and the mark now say the same thing.
const PRACTICE = 'I design and I build the systems other teams build against.';
---

<Layout
  title="Résumé — Andrew Shiau"
  description="Andrew Shiau — frontend engineer. Design systems at Amazon, Composer and Luthier, Microsoft Office prototyping. One page."
>
  <div class="grid">
    <!-- The panel, hand-rolled for the reason in the frontmatter. Same shape as every case study:
         the mark and wordmark as the link home, the title, then a ledger read before the content.
         The mark is the WHOLE figure — this page is both halves of the practice, which is the one
         claim a résumé for this particular job has to make in its first 60px. -->
    <div class="pin">
      <p class="smark">
        <a class="smark-home" href="/"><MarkFigure side="both" />Andrew Shiau</a>
      </p>
      <h1>Résumé</h1>

      <div class="facts">
        <div class="frow">
          <div class="fm">Now</div>
          <div class="ftx">Frontend engineer, <b>Shopping Design Tech</b> at Amazon. Design
            systems and the tooling that builds against them.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="frow">
          <div class="fm">Both halves</div>
          <div class="ftx">I design and I build. The two procedures I run are written down at
            <a href="/method/">/method/</a>.</div>
        </div>
        <!-- The contact row EARNS its place on this page, unlike on a case study (where it was
             deleted — see PanelHead.astro). A résumé's whole function is to produce a reply, so
             the address is the point of the document rather than an ask appended to an argument.
             Same reasoning that keeps it on the index and on /gate/. -->
        <div class="frow">
          <div class="fm">Contact</div>
          <div class="ftx"><a href="mailto:hi@andrewshiau.com">hi@andrewshiau.com</a><br
            /><a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/halfcadence/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">linkedin</a>
            <a href="https://github.com/halfcadence" target="_blank" rel="noopener">github</a></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="splitrule" aria-hidden="true"></div>

    <div class="feed">
      <div class="grid">
        <p class="dek c1-12">
          Frontend engineer, seven years in. Design systems and the tools teams build with —
          Amazon’s marketing-graphics pipeline, the Amazon app’s design tokens, and now the
          tooling for one shopping surface many teams ship to.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- PRINT ONLY: the address, repeated at the top of the paper version. On screen it is in
           the panel to the left, which does not exist on paper — the print stylesheet collapses
           the split to one column, and a résumé whose contact details are 40cm down the page is
           a résumé nobody replies to. `aria-hidden` and `display:none` on screen, so a screen
           reader never hears the same three links twice.

           THE NAME GETS ITS OWN LINE (chooser: resume-pdf-options, Q2/02). It was the first twelve
           characters of what is otherwise a list of five URLs, at body size and body weight — so in
           a stack of PDFs there was nothing to find. Weight 500 is the site's one emphasis weight
           and 500 is already on this page, which is also why it is safe in print: see the section-
           head comment below for what happens when print asks for a weight the screen never does.
           No new size. The URLs drop to `--faint`.

           AND THE ADDRESSES ARE REAL ANCHORS (Q5/01). The produced PDF had `/Link: 0, /URI: 0,
           /Annots: 0` — verified in the file, with a control page proving Chrome does emit link
           annotations on this path. Every anchor lived in `.smark` or `.pin .facts`, both hidden in
           print, so the only contact information reaching paper was this line, as plain text. On
           the ⌘P → Save as PDF → email path — the actual path — the recipient could not click the
           email address. The URLs are already spelled out here, so wrapping them costs nothing
           visually: `.printhead a` is black with no underline, and its `::after` URL-printing is
           suppressed because the text IS the URL. -->
      <div class="printhead" aria-hidden="true">
        <span class="phname">Andrew Shiau</span>
        <span class="phrole">Frontend engineer · Seattle · <a href="mailto:hi@andrewshiau.com">hi@andrewshiau.com</a></span>
        {/* `{' '}` around each separator, not a raw newline. JSX collapses a newline between two
            elements to nothing, so the PDF read `andrewshiau.com ·linkedin.com/…` — the same class
            of bug as the glued `form,engineering` earlier in this rebuild, and again invisible in
            the source because the indentation looks like a space. */}
        <span class="phlinks"><a href="https://andrewshiau.com">andrewshiau.com</a>{' · '}<a
          href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/halfcadence/">linkedin.com/in/halfcadence</a>{' · '}<a
          href="https://github.com/halfcadence">github.com/halfcadence</a></span>
      </div>

      {/* The practice row: a `.rsect` with no head, holding one role-shaped row. See the `PRACTICE`
           comment in the frontmatter. It takes no section interval on paper because it belongs to
           the head block above it rather than opening a new division. */}
      <div class="rsect practice">
        <div class="role">
          <div class="rspan">Practice</div>
          <div class="rbody"><div class="rtitle">{PRACTICE}</div></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      {/* ── ONE SECTION, ONE GRID (chooser: resume-pdf-options, Q3/03 — "the head hangs in the date
           column"). The head is a sibling of the roles inside a single `.rsect` grid rather than its
           own full-width `.grid.sect` block, which is what lets it share the hung axis with the
           dates instead of sitting on a line of its own.

           WHAT I DREW IN THE CHOOSER WAS WORSE THAN THIS, and the difference matters. That render
           put the section name in the label column and pushed EVERY date inline beside its location
           — which reads fine in a 14-line crop and quietly destroys the one thing a reader uses to
           scan a CV: a single column of dates you can run your eye down. The option's label didn't
           say that, because I hadn't noticed I'd done it.
           So the label column STACKS instead: the section name, then the date, both hanging on the
           same character. Column 2 gets the org on the head's row. Nothing moves inline, the date
           axis survives intact, and the head still costs no line of its own. */}
      <div class="rsect">
        <h2 class="rsecth">Experience</h2>
        {ROLES.map((r) => (
          /* `display:contents` on `.role` so the span and the body become direct children of the
             section grid and land in its two columns. Without it each role would be one grid item
             in column 1 and the axis would collapse. */
          <div class="role">
            <div class="rspan">{r.span}</div>
            <div class="rbody">
              <h3 class="rorg">{r.org}</h3>
              <div class="rtitle">{r.title}{r.where && <span class="rwhere"> · {r.where}</span>}</div>
              {r.note && <p class="rnote">{r.note}</p>}
              <ul class="rlines">
                {r.lines.map((l) => <li>{l}</li>)}
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      {/* Still two `.role` blocks, NOT the one folded row the chooser sketched — and the reason is
          worth recording, because it reverses part of Q1/02. Folding both degrees into a single
          hung row saves 124px of paper, and it buries the one fact on this page a reader repeats to
          a colleague: a CS degree and a cello performance degree, taken together. That is the line
          that makes the "I design and I build" claim at the top of the page biographical instead of
          rhetorical, and a run-in row of four clauses hides it. The 124px came out of the Stack
          section instead, where the words were genuinely doing nothing. Cut the padding, not the
          evidence. */}
      <div class="rsect">
        <h2 class="rsecth">Education</h2>
        {EDUCATION.map((e) => (
          <div class="role">
            <div class="rspan">{e.span}</div>
            <div class="rbody">
              <h3 class="rorg">{e.org}</h3>
              <div class="rtitle">{e.what}</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      {/* ── STACK IS TWO ROWS, NOT THREE, AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES IT ONE PAGE.
           The arithmetic is exact and worth stating, because it is the whole reason for the shape:
           the printed content box is 713pt and the leading is 14.7pt, so a sheet holds 48.5 lines.
           Page 1 was carrying 48 — full — with three lines spilling. No amount of tightening moves
           a line onto a full page, so the last three lines had to leave, and Stack was where they
           could go without losing a fact.
           `Agents` folds into `Design`: Claude Code IS a design tool here, and a row of its own for
           one clause was a label doing the work of a comma. `Build` and `Design` keep their rows
           because the two halves of the practice are the page's argument — collapsing those into one
           `Tools` row would save a line and delete the distinction the whole site is about.
           Two rows, three lines saved, nothing a reader wanted is gone. */}
      <div class="rsect stack">
        <h2 class="rsecth">Stack</h2>
        <div class="role">
          <div class="rspan">Build</div>
          <div class="rbody"><div class="rtitle">{BUILD}</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="role">
          <div class="rspan">Design</div>
          <div class="rbody"><div class="rtitle">{DESIGN}</div></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- The close: how to get the paper copy. It is one sentence rather than a button, because
           the mechanism IS the browser's own — there is nothing to click that Cmd-P doesn't do
           better, and a "Download PDF" link would either point at a file that drifts from this
           page or fire a script to make one. `.kb` / `.tou` is the site's existing device for an
           affordance a touch device doesn't have; a phone gets the fact without the keystroke. -->
      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="facts">
          <div class="frow">
            <div class="fm">On paper</div>
            <div class="ftx">
              <span class="kb">This page is set for print. Press <code>⌘P</code> and save as
                PDF — two pages, black on white.</span>
              <span class="tou">This page is set for print: print it from your browser to get
                the PDF, two pages, black on white.</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style is:global>
  /* ── THE RÉSUMÉ'S OWN DEVICE. Scoped here rather than added to global.css because exactly one
     page emits it: a `.role` is a dated block with a hung span and a bulleted body, and nothing
     else on the site is shaped like that. If a second page ever needs it, it moves. */
  /* The interval above each role is a RULE, not an inline style on the wrapper. It was
     `style="margin-top:var(--group)"` on every `.grid` holding a role, and an inline margin cannot
     be overridden by the print block without `!important` — which is exactly the trap
     `.grid:has(> .block)` in global.css was written to get out of. Stated here, print can compress
     it, and the markup carries no numbers. */
  /* ── THE SECTION IS THE GRID (Q3/03). One two-column grid per section, holding the head and every
     role in it, so all of them share ONE hung axis. `.role` is `display:contents` so its span and
     body become direct children of that grid and land in its two tracks — a nested grid per role
     would give each role its own axis, which is the thing this replaces.
     `1 / -1` on the section, not a literal track count: it sits inside `.feed`, whose grid is
     narrower than the page's, and a literal number here is the phantom-measure bug that cost this
     rebuild four passes. */
  .rsect{grid-column:1 / -1;display:grid;grid-template-columns:var(--label) 1fr;
    column-gap:var(--gutter);margin-top:var(--sect)}
  .rsect:first-of-type{margin-top:0}
  /* The head hangs in the label column on the same row as the first role's org. `--ink` and weight
     500, which is the site's one emphasis — no caps, no second size, no rule. */
  .rsecth{grid-column:1;font-weight:500;color:var(--ink);margin:0}
  .role{display:contents}
  /* Every role after the first takes the interval; the first sits on the head's row. */
  .rsect > .role + .role > .rspan,
  .rsect > .role + .role > .rbody{margin-top:var(--group)}
  /* The span hangs in the label column, in `--faint`, like every other hung label on the site. */
  .role .rspan{grid-column:1;color:var(--faint)}
  .role .rbody{grid-column:2;max-width:62ch}
  /* The org is the fact; `h3` is already reset to the body size and weight site-wide (see the
     `h3,h4,h5,h6` rule in global.css), so this only adds the weight that makes it the line the
     eye lands on. Weight, not size and not colour — the page has one size, and colour means
     interaction. */
  .role .rorg{font-weight:500;color:var(--ink)}
  .role .rtitle{color:var(--dim)}
  .role .rwhere{color:var(--faint)}
  .role .rnote{color:var(--dim);margin:var(--tight) 0 0}
  /* The bullets. `--tight` above the list binds it to the title above it; `list-style:none` with
     a hung `–` because a browser's own bullet sits off the 182px content axis and the whole page
     is built on that axis holding. `1.5ch` is the dash plus its space at this size. */
  .role .rlines{list-style:none;margin:var(--tight) 0 0;padding:0}
  .role .rlines li{position:relative;padding-left:1.5ch;color:var(--dim)}
  .role .rlines li::before{content:"–";position:absolute;left:0;color:var(--faint)}
  .role .rlines li + li{margin-top:var(--tight)}
  @media(max-width:1060px){
    /* Stacks with every other hung device at the same width and for the same reason: below 1060
       the label column can no longer hold its own label. The section grid collapses to one column
       and `.role` goes back to being a block, so the head, the date and the body all stack. */
    .rsect{grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);column-gap:0}
    .role{display:block}
    .role .rspan{color:var(--ink);margin-bottom:var(--tight)}
    .rsect > .role + .role > .rspan,
    .rsect > .role + .role > .rbody{margin-top:0}
    .rsect > .role + .role{margin-top:var(--group)}
    .rsecth{margin-bottom:var(--group)}
  }

  /* The print-only address line is hidden on screen. */
  .printhead{display:none}
  /* ── AND SO IS THE PRACTICE ROW, for the same reason and it is worth stating: it exists to
     replace what PRINT deletes. On screen the panel's "Both halves" row already says "I design and
     I build" and the dek says the longer version — so shown on screen the row is the third
     paraphrase of one sentence in 400px, and it opened with the panel's exact five words. Print
     hides both of those, which is the gap the row was written for. One statement per medium. */
  .rsect.practice{display:none}


  /* ── THE PAPER VERSION. The annotation asked for "a 1 page site which can be exported to a 1-2
     page pdf", and this is the export: no committed PDF, no generator, no dependency — the page
     re-sets itself for paper and the browser does the rest.
     What has to change for paper, and why each one is a real defect rather than a preference:
     - The SPLIT has to collapse. The panel is `position:sticky` inside a viewport-height box; on
       paper there is no viewport to stick to, so it would print once at the top and leave the
       contact details on page 1 while the roles run to page 2. One column, and the panel's
       content prints as the document's head.
     - COLOUR goes to black on white. The site's paper is #f4f3ef and its ink is #141412; a warm
       ground costs a full ink cartridge and a résumé is the one page that gets printed by someone
       else, on someone else's printer.
     - LINKS lose their underline colour and print their URL, because a printed link that says
       "linkedin" is a dead end. `content:" (" attr(href) ")"` only on the ones that need it —
       mailto: and internal paths are already legible as text.
     - The GRID OVERLAY and the scroll-progress bar are interactive furniture and must not print.

     ── HOW THIS WAS ACTUALLY MEASURED, after getting it wrong once. The first pass tuned the scale
     against a number from a screen iframe with the print rules copied into it, concluded "1.90
     pages", and shipped. The real export was THREE pages. The simulation could not have been right:
     an iframe has no `@page`, no `break-inside`, and no pagination at all — it reports one tall
     column, so dividing its height by a page height ignores every break Chrome actually inserts.
     Two of the three defects the owner saw were invisible to it.
     The loop that replaced it, and the reason it can fail: print the page with headless Chrome
     (`--print-to-pdf`) over a LOOPBACK server, then read the PDF back — page count from the file
     itself, and the text of each page to see where the breaks landed. `scripts/print-check.py`
     does it in one command. A server is required rather than convenient: the built CSS and the four
     Plex woff2 files are referenced root-absolute (`/_astro/…`, `/fonts/…`), which `file://` cannot
     resolve, so a `file://` render silently substitutes a system sans — which is most of why the
     downloaded PDF looked, in the owner's words, "uglier than the site". 127.0.0.1 only, foreground,
     torn down in a `finally`.

     ── WHAT THE REAL PDF SHOWED, and what each fix is for:
     1. EVERY DATE WRAPPED. "Aug 2025 – present" broke after the dash on all six roles. Cause: the
        site's `.feed .grid > .c1-12:has(> h2:only-child) > h2` clamps a lone section head to
        `width:var(--label)`, and `--label` is derived from the VIEWPORT (`min(100vw,--measure)`) —
        which in a print context computes against the paper box and collapses the column. `.rspan`
        inherited a 26mm track from the same thinking. Both are stated in `ch` now: a date is a
        known string, so its column is measured in characters, and `white-space:nowrap` asserts it
        rather than hoping.
     2. PAGE 1 ENDED HALF-EMPTY. `break-inside:avoid` on `.role` is right for a 3-line job and
        wrong for the 7-bullet Composer role, which is taller than the space left — so Chrome moved
        the whole block to page 2 and left ~40% of page 1 blank. It is `auto` now, with orphan and
        widow control instead: a role may split, but never with fewer than two lines on either side
        of the fold, and its heading never sits alone at the foot of a page.
     3. THE HEADS WERE INVISIBLE AS HEADS. `h2{font-weight:600}` on paper is one step from the 500
        of a job title, so "Experience" and "Education" read as another line of the list. They take
        a hairline rule and the small-caps treatment the screen gives a section, which is the one
        piece of chrome paper genuinely needs — a reader scanning a printout has no scrollbar to
        tell them where they are.
     10.5pt at 1.4 stays; the scale (4/9/14) survived the re-measure. */
  @media print{
    :root{
      --paper:#fff; --ink:#000; --dim:#222; --faint:#555; --line:#bbb; --accent:#000;
      --step:10.5pt; --lead:1.4;
      --tight:4pt; --group:9pt; --sect:14pt; --unit:9pt; --gutter:12pt;
    }
    @page{margin:14mm 16mm}
    html,body{background:#fff;color:#000;font-size:10.5pt;line-height:1.4}
    /* `.wrap`'s 28px side padding is the screen's margin; on paper `@page` owns the margin, and
       keeping both cost 56px of the 695px content box. */
    .wrap{padding:0 !important;max-width:none !important}
    /* `.wrap` carries `padding-bottom:calc(var(--unit)*5)` for the screen — 45pt of trailing space
       that, on paper, is a box with height after the last line of text. It was pushing a BLANK
       second sheet: the print check reported "last page carries 0 lines", which is the one failure
       mode a page count alone would call two pages and a human would call a printer error. */
    .wrap{padding-bottom:0 !important}
    /* The section interval is stated on `.grid.sect` for the screen; on paper the head's own
       `margin-top` carries it, so leaving both stacked two intervals — the same non-collapsing
       grid-item defect this pass fixed on `.block`. */
    .grid.sect{margin-top:0 !important}
    .grid:has(> .role){margin-top:var(--group) !important}
    /* The split collapses: both children become full-width blocks in source order, so the panel
       (name, title, contact) prints as the head and the feed follows. */
    .grid,.grid.split{display:block !important}
    .pin,.feed{display:block !important;position:static !important;
      max-height:none !important;overflow:visible !important;width:auto !important}
    .splitrule,.guide,.progress,.colo-rule{display:none !important}
    /* The panel's own head: on paper the wordmark link and the h1 "Résumé" are two lines saying
       what the reader already knows they are holding, so the mark goes and the printhead line
       carries the name and the address instead. */
    .smark,.pin h1{display:none !important}
    .pin .facts{display:none !important}
    /* `max-width:none` because `p{max-width:62ch}` in global.css is a SCREEN measure and this is
       the one element on the printed page that runs the full width. The paper's measure is 83ch
       on Letter, so the address line was wrapping 21 characters early and spending a third line
       on the 22 characters of `github.com/halfcadence`. Two lines now (73ch + 52ch), still
       inside the 45–75 comfort band, and the reclaimed line is what takes page 2 from 99% full
       to 92% — which is the difference between a two-page document and a two-page document that
       becomes three the next time a bullet is added. */
    .printhead{display:block !important;margin:0 0 4pt;color:#000;
      max-width:none !important}
    /* THE NAME ON ITS OWN LINE (Q2/02). Weight 500, no new size — and 500 rather than 600 for the
       mechanical reason in the section-head comment below: it is already on this page, so the face
       is cached when print layout runs. */
    .printhead .phname{display:block;font-weight:500}
    .printhead .phrole{display:block}
    .printhead .phlinks{display:block;color:#555}
    /* THE ANCHORS ARE INVISIBLE ON PAPER (Q5/01). The text is already the URL, so the `::after`
       that prints a destination would print it twice, and an underline would add a mark that means
       nothing where nothing is clickable. What survives into the PDF is the link ANNOTATION, which
       is the whole point: the emailed copy is clickable and the printed copy is unchanged. */
    .printhead a{color:#000 !important;text-decoration:none !important}
    .printhead .phlinks a{color:#555 !important}
    .printhead a::after{content:none !important}
    .dek{margin-top:var(--tight)}
    /* ── THE SECTION HEAD HANGS IN THE DATE COLUMN (chooser: resume-pdf-options, Q3/03).
       It was 9pt uppercase tracked `.14em` at weight 600 with a hairline over it: FOUR devices the
       mono pass deleted from the screen, revived for paper. The argument for them was real — paper
       has no colour and no scrollbar, so a reader has no other way to know where they are — but it
       was answering the wrong question. This page already HAS a mechanism for an annotation that
       isn't body copy: the hung column the dates sit in. Putting the head there means the printed
       document has ONE axis carrying every annotation — section names, dates, education years, stack
       labels, all starting at the same character — and needs no second size, no caps, no tracking
       and no rule to say "a division starts here". One mechanism per job, which is the rule the
       caps treatment was breaking.
       `width:auto` stays and is still load-bearing: the site clamps a lone `h2` to `--label`, which
       is derived from `min(100vw, --measure)` and collapses in a print context. Every head has to
       be told to be its own width.
       ── AND THE WEIGHT IS 500, NOT 600, BECAUSE 600 PRINTED IN THE WRONG TYPEFACE. Kept here
       because it is the reason this whole question was worth asking, and because the trap is
       reusable: a weight requested ONLY by a print stylesheet is never fetched during screen
       layout, and print layout does not wait for a font. The SemiBold face wasn't cached, so the
       three heads fell through `--mono` to the generic `monospace` tail and Chrome embedded
       `BAAAAA+DejaVuSansMono-Bold` in the PDF beside two IBM Plex faces — verified in the
       artifact's own font table, on the page whose whole argument is one typeface. 500 is the fix
       rather than a `<link rel=preload>` because 500 is ALREADY on this page (`.role .rorg`), so
       the face is always cached: nothing to preload, nothing to keep in sync. `scripts/print-check.py`
       now fails on any embedded face that isn't IBM Plex Mono, so this cannot recur silently.
       A DEPARTURE FROM THE SYSTEM TURNED OUT TO HAVE A MECHANICAL COST, NOT JUST AN AESTHETIC ONE,
       which is the strongest argument the system has ever made for itself here. */
    /* The section grid, in `ch` rather than `--label` — that token is derived from the viewport and
       collapses on paper, which is the root cause of three separate defects in this file's history.
       20ch is the longest span ("Feb 2025 – May 2025", 19 characters) plus one. */
    .rsect{display:grid !important;grid-template-columns:20ch 1fr;column-gap:2ch;
      margin-top:var(--sect)}
    .rsect:first-of-type{margin-top:0}
    /* ── THE HEAD SHARES ITS ROW WITH THE FIRST ROLE, which is the point of hanging it and is also
       the last line of the one-page fix. `grid-row:1` puts the head in the label column of the same
       row the first role's date would occupy, and that date moves to the row below with its body —
       so the section name is what hangs beside the first org, and no row is half empty.
       Measured: 714pt against a 713pt page before this, i.e. over by ONE POINT. Three section heads
       each held a row whose body column was blank; reclaiming them is 3 lines for 1pt of need, which
       is the margin this document has lacked at every step. */
    /* ── THE HEAD KEEPS ITS OWN ROW, and two attempts to reclaim it are recorded because both
       failed in instructive ways:
       - Explicit `grid-row` on the head and the first role's cells unpaired each label from its
         value (`Build` and its list on separate lines) and made the document LONGER. A
         `display:contents` role relies on the grid's auto-placement to keep its two cells on one
         row; overriding placement breaks exactly that.
       - A negative `margin-bottom` on the head did nothing: a grid row sizes to its content, so
         pulling an item up inside its own row does not remove the row.
       Reclaiming it properly means the head and the first org sharing one row, which needs them to
       be siblings in the same row — a markup change, not a CSS one, and not worth it for the 1pt
       this document was over. The point came out of the type instead: see `--sect` below. */
    .rsecth{grid-column:1;font-weight:500;font-size:inherit;letter-spacing:0;
      text-transform:none;margin:0;padding:0;border:0;width:auto !important;
      max-width:none !important}
    /* A ROLE MAY SPLIT, but not carelessly (defect 2). `break-inside:avoid` shunted the 7-bullet
       Composer role to its own page and left page 1 40% empty. Orphans/widows do the job properly:
       two lines minimum on either side of a fold, so a job can continue across pages the way prose
       does, and `break-after:avoid` on its heading keeps the org name from sitting alone at the
       bottom of a sheet.
       The role is `display:contents` now, so it is not itself a box and cannot carry
       `break-inside` — the properties move to the section grid and to the cells. */
    .role{display:contents !important}
    .rsect{orphans:2;widows:2}
    /* ── THE INTERVAL BETWEEN ROLES IS 6pt ON PAPER, NOT 9, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE ONE-PAGE FIX.
       Measured before deciding: the document was 816pt of content against a 713pt page — 1.14
       pages, over by 103pt. Of that overflow, 81pt was the nine 9pt gaps between roles and 28pt was
       the two 14pt section gaps: 109pt of pure vertical space, against 103pt of overflow. So the
       second sheet existed to hold the spacing, not the content.
       The screen keeps `--group` at 9pt because a screen has infinite paper and the interval is
       doing real work there. On a sheet with a hard edge, 6pt between two blocks that already have
       a hung date to separate them is enough — the axis is the separator, the space is only
       reinforcement. Same reasoning as the mobile space compression: a scale should start one step
       lower where the medium is tighter, which is what having a scale is for.
       This is also why the cut came first and the spacing second. Tightening space to rescue copy
       that shouldn't be there would have been the wrong order — the 217 words were genuinely not
       carrying anything, and only after they were gone was the remaining gap small enough that
       6pt closed it honestly. */
    .rsect > .role + .role > .rspan,
    .rsect > .role + .role > .rbody{margin-top:4pt}
    /* 8pt, and the last two points of a 714pt document on a 713pt page came from here. The section
       interval is the least load-bearing space on the sheet: a section already announces itself with
       a name in the hung column, so the gap above it is reinforcement, not the signal. Two sections
       have a gap, so 10 → 8 is 4pt for 1pt of need. */
    .rsect{margin-top:5pt}
    /* ── THE LAST SECTION IS ATOMIC, because `break-after:avoid` on the head did not hold it to its
       rows. The head is a grid ITEM, and Chrome applies `break-after` to a row it is in rather than
       binding it to the next row's content — so `Stack` printed alone at the foot of page 1 with its
       two rows on page 2. A widowed heading is the exact defect that rule exists to prevent, and it
       silently failed under the new structure.
       `break-inside:avoid` on the section is the fix that actually holds: the section moves as one
       object, head and rows together, which is what a reader needs and what the rule intended. */
    .rsect{break-inside:avoid;page-break-inside:avoid}
    /* The practice row has no head, so nothing can be widowed, and it belongs to the head block
       above rather than opening a division — no interval, and free to break. */
    .rsect.practice{display:grid !important;break-inside:auto;page-break-inside:auto;
      margin-top:0}
    /* ── `Stack` KEEPS ITS HEAD, and the attempt to delete it is recorded because it looked right
       and printed wrong. The argument was sound — `Build` and `Design` hang in the label column and
       already name themselves, so a `Stack` head above them is a label for two labels — and it did
       buy the line the practice row needed. But in the artifact the two rows then read as unlabelled
       continuations of EDUCATION, because the only thing separating a section from the one above it
       on this sheet is its name in the hung column. Remove the name and the section stops existing.
       The line came out of the role interval instead (4pt, below). */
    /* With no head, Stack has no widow to protect, so it must not be atomic: an unbreakable block
       makes Chrome reserve its full height on page 1 even when the content fits, which is what kept
       buying a second sheet. Two short rows can split without harm. */
    .rsect.stack{break-inside:auto;page-break-inside:auto}
    /* `orphans:2` inherited from `.rsect` forces two lines to travel together; Stack's rows are
       single lines that may sit alone. */
    .rsect.stack{orphans:1;widows:1}
    /* 20ch, COUNTED, not guessed — and the first cut of this rule guessed 13ch and was wrong in a
       way worth recording, because `nowrap` is what made it visible. The longest span on the page is
       "Feb 2025 – May 2025" / "Nov 2018 – Jun 2019" at 19 characters; at 13ch the nowrap text ran
       straight out of its cell and collided with the org name — the print check screenshot read
       "Aug 2025 – preseAmazon". That is the assertion doing its job: a wrapped date hides in a
       layout, an overlapping one cannot be missed.
       20ch = 19 plus one, and the arithmetic says it fits: the content box is 7.24in = 521pt, and
       Plex Mono's advance at 10.5pt is 6.3pt, so the line is 82 characters — 20 for the date, 2 for
       the gutter, 60 left for the body. Stated in `ch` because in a monospace face a character IS
       the unit, so a column measured in characters cannot drift when the type size changes. */
    .role .rspan{color:#555;white-space:nowrap}
    /* ── `break-after:avoid` ON `.rorg` / `.rtitle` IS DELETED, and it was costing 5 lines.
       It was written when `.role` was its own grid and the org/title were block siblings of the
       bullet list, where "don't break after the org name" is exactly right — it stops "Amazon"
       sitting alone at the foot of a sheet. Under Q3/03 the role is `display:contents` and those
       two are inside a single grid CELL (`.rbody`) with the bullets. `break-after` on a box inside
       a cell can't move a fragmentation point that belongs to the grid ROW, so the declaration no
       longer does the job it was written for — but Chrome still honours it as a constraint on the
       row, which is how it ended up forcing an EARLIER break than necessary.
       Measured by bisection, one declaration at a time against a control: with it, page 1 carried
       43 lines and page 2 carried 12. Without it, 48 and 7 — five lines moved up and nothing else
       changed. The widow it was written to prevent cannot occur anyway, because the org, title and
       bullets are now one cell and a cell is not split between its own children here.
       A rule that was correct for the old structure became a pessimisation under the new one. Worth
       recording because nothing about it looked wrong: it read as a safety rule, it had a good
       comment, and it was silently buying a second sheet. */
    .role .rbody{max-width:none}
    .role .rlines li{break-inside:avoid;page-break-inside:avoid}
    .frow{break-inside:avoid;page-break-inside:avoid}
    /* Links: black, no underline colour games.
       ── THE URL-PRINTING RULE IS DELETED, and it was dead code with a paragraph defending it.
       `a[target="_blank"]::after{content:" (" attr(href) ")"}` could never match: every external
       anchor on this page lives in `.smark` or `.pin .facts`, both `display:none` here, so the
       produced PDF had `/Link: 0, /URI: 0, /Annots: 0`. The comment above it described a document
       that did not exist. The real fix was upstream — the printhead's addresses are anchors now
       (see `.printhead a`), which is what makes the emailed copy clickable. Its companion
       `a[target="_blank"]::before{content:none}` went too, and was doubly dead: the site puts the
       ↗ on `::after`, not `::before`, so it was aimed at the wrong pseudo-element AND at elements
       that were not on the page. */
    a{color:#000 !important;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:#999 !important}
    /* The keystroke instruction is screen furniture — you are already holding the output. */
    .kb,.tou{display:none !important}
    /* …and so is the row that holds it: "On paper" tells a reader of the paper copy how to make
       the paper copy. Deleting the whole `.frow` rather than just its text is what takes the
       document under two pages — it was the last block, and a trailing empty label row would have
       pushed a third sheet on its own. */
    .frow:has(.kb){display:none !important}
    /* The dek is the screen's opening paragraph. On paper the printhead line above it already
       states the name and the role, and a résumé's first inch belongs to the first job. */
    .dek{display:none !important}
    /* The `.facts` ledger rules that used to live here are gone: Stack is a `.rsect` now, like
       Experience and Education, so it hangs its label on the one axis by construction instead of
       by a `20ch` restatement that had to be kept in sync with `.role`'s. Three sections, one
       grid, one number. What survives is the rule for the one remaining `.facts` block — the
       "On paper" row — which print deletes. */
    .feed .facts .frow:has(.kb){display:none !important}
  }
</style>
